  "step_2": "The verbal report indicates that the individual considered all information and explicitly noted that Lottery B offers a guaranteed (sure) win, even though it is lower than the potential win in Lottery A (which yields 4000 Euros in 4 out of 5 cases). The individual acknowledges the sure outcome in B and contrasts it with the higher but uncertain gain in A. Despite recognizing the lower value of the guaranteed outcome, the individual still chose B. The justification centers on the comparison between the guaranteed gain in B and the probabilistic (but higher) gain in A.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason prefers the lottery *without* a sure outcome (i.e., Lottery A). However, the individual chose B, which has a sure outcome. More importantly, the individual’s deliberation explicitly weighs the sure outcome in B against the uncertain but higher potential of A, suggesting that the presence of a sure outcome in B was a key consideration — likely a reason to favor B. This is opposite to the rule of the decision reason, which would favor A *because* it lacks a sure outcome. The individual appears to value the sure outcome (even while acknowledging its lower amount), which contradicts the logic of the given decision reason. Therefore, the individual did not use this reason; in fact, their reasoning moves in the opposite direction.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
